 "desks": {
  "news": "Lead with the verbal sentence and the fact: who, what, where, when, in the first 25 words. Name the source and the day (\"أمس الأربعاء\"). Keep sentences short; no adjectives of praise; attribute with قال/أعلنت/أفادت/وفق.",
  "business": "Sober, numerate and precise: figure, unit, period and source in every paragraph (مليون يورو, 6%, الربع الثالث). Name the company, the regulator and the law; use \"ارتفع/تراجع/بلغ\" rather than \"شهد نموًا ملحوظًا\". Explain jargon once.",
  "property_legal": "Law and property explainers need the statute, the article, the authority, the deadline, the fee and the exception, in that order. Use \"يشترط/يجب/يحق\" for obligations and rights; never estate-agent hype; state what is not legal advice in one line.",
  "relocation_guide": "Practical and stepwise but written as prose or numbered steps with real details: the office, the form, the days, the cost in euros. Gulf and Levant readers want residency, tax and schooling facts first; avoid dream language like \"ابدأ حياة جديدة\".",
  "economy": "Analytical, with a thesis in the first paragraph, two or three figures, and a named economist or institution (البنك المركزي القبرصي، يوروستات). Explain cause and effect with verbs, not \"مما يعكس\". End on what to watch next, with a date.",
  "culture": "Elegant and informed: name the work, the artist, the venue, the year and the context; one precise judgement in plain words. Avoid \"تحفة فنية\" and \"يأسر القلوب\"; let the detail do the praising.",
  "food": "Sensory but concrete: the dish, the ingredient, the cook, the price and the address. One vivid verb per paragraph (يُنضج، يُقدَّم، يفوح), no \"نكهات غنية\" or \"يرضي جميع الأذواق\".",
  "fashion_lifestyle": "Confident, short sentences in the voice of Vogue Arabia: the designer, the fabric, the cut, the price and the shop. Avoid \"إطلالة متكاملة\" and \"أناقة لا تضاهى\"; replace with a named piece and why it matters.",
  "travel": "Place-first and useful: the town, the road, the season, the hours, the price. Use the reader's senses once, then the practical detail (distance in km, flight time from Dubai or Beirut). No \"رحلة عبر الزمن\" or \"جنة على الأرض\".",
  "events": "Who, what, where, when and how much, in the first paragraph; the full programme afterwards. Give exact dates, times, ticket prices and a booking link or phone number; never \"حدث لا يفوّت\".",
  "interview": "Open on a scene or a sharp quote, introduce the person with one concrete detail, and let the answers carry the voice. Use قال/أجاب/أضاف and a short, truthful framing; keep the interviewee's own words intact.",
  "review": "Give a verdict early and defend it with specifics: what worked, what did not, the price and who should go. Use measured Arabic (\"جيد\", \"مقنع\", \"دون المتوقع\") rather than \"تحفة\" or \"مذهل\"; no rating without reasons.",
  "people": "Profile through a scene, a decision and a number: the career turn, the company, the family or street, the date. Avoid \"قصة نجاح ملهمة\" and hagiography; show the person through what they did and said."
 }
